 REMOVED_CASE_ID  MASTROMATTEI SUSAN A 70 F  REG REF 04 25    REMOVED_CASE_ID   WIC MELNICK AVROHM N  M D                                                                                             Specimen   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   REMOVED_CASE_ID  SOUT Received  04 25 05  REMOVED_CASE_ID  Spec Type    Clinical diagnosis   history  5mm  cluster of microcalcifications of left upper inner quadrant  positive family history of breast cancer  sister   65 years     ductal carcinoma in situ vs fibrocystic changes Edited by  REMOVED_DATE    REMOVED_CASE_ID  PAT MURJ Gross description  The specimen is received in formalin in one container  labeled  stereotactic breast biopsies  left breast  and consists of two cassettes  The first  which is purple in color and designated on the specimen requisition to be the upper inner quadrant with calcifications  consists of four yellowish red needle biopsies measuring 1 4cm  in length x 0 3cm  in diameter to 2 5cm  in length x 0 3cm  in diameter  4X all  labeled 1  The second  which is yellow in color and designated on the specimen requisition to be the remainder of the tissue  consists of a 2 x 2 x 0 4cm  aggregate of yellowish red  firm needle biopsies measuring 0 3cm  in diameter each  1 agg  all  labeled 2  Dictated by  LV Edited by  REMOVED_DATE    REMOVED_CASE_ID  PAT MURJ DIAGNOSIS  Left breast  upper inner quadrant  needle core biopsies  Foci suspicious for microinvasive carcinoma  see note   Focal atypical ductal hyperplasia and atypical lobular hyperplasia  Fibrocystic changes with focal microcalcifications  Edited by  REMOVED_DATE    REMOVED_CASE_ID  PAT DORD DESCRIPTIVE COMMENT  NOTE  There are multiple microscopic foci of irregularly placed  small cords of epithelial cells which are suspicious for invasive carcinoma  Immunohistochemical staining for keratin is positive in these cells  the cells are positive for E cadherin focally but also focally some of the cells are negative  The cells are positive for estrogen receptors and negative for progesterone receptors  The slides were sent for consultation to Dr  Frederick Koerner at Massachusetts General Hospital who concurs and who states the following in his consult letter   The glandular tissue in the background of this core biopsy specimen appears atrophic  I do not seen anything in the hematoxylin eosin stained sections that I would classify as atypical ductal hyperplasia  but a duct in the E cadherin stained section of block  1  does contain a proliferation of atypical ductal cells  As you point out  the more important question centers on the nature of the small  bland cells growing individually and in small clusters and permeating the stroma in several foci  The immunohistochemical stains for keratin highlight the number of these cells and their permeative pattern  The cells possess slightly enlarged nuclei  homogeneous chromatin  small nucleoli  and ample  pale cytoplasm  The cytological characteristics of the cells give them a histiocytoid appearance  and the immunohistochemical stains for E cadherin show that certain of the cells do not stain for the protein  whereas others stain only weakly  These findings suggest that the suspicious cells probably represent a type of invasive lobular carcinoma  Because of the small number of the cells in the specimen and the subtle nature of their atypicality  I do not feel completely confident making this diagnosis  instead  I would classify the cells as suspicious for invasive carcinoma  Moreover  I would urge the patient and her physician to undergo an excision of this region of the left breast and I feel so strongly about the likelihood of the presence of DESCRIPTIVE COMMENT   Continued  invasive carcinoma that I would also suggest that the surgeon consider performing a sentinel lymph node biopsy  Final Diagnosis by  Daniel D  Koelliker  M D  Edited by  REMOVED_DATE    REMOVED_CASE_ID  PAT DORD REMOVED_DATE    REMOVED_CASE_ID  MD KOED REMOVED_DATE    REMOVED_CASE_ID  PAT DORD REMOVED_DATE    REMOVED_CASE_ID  MD KOED Notes  REMOVED_DATE  Received 8 slides and report from MGH via mail   mi  CR  REMOVED_DATE  Dr  Koerner s report rec d  djd REMOVED_DATE  Sent eight slides  1 2L and 3L  1 E cad   1 Pank   2 1L and 2L  2 Pank  and 2 E cad   to Dr  Frederick Koerner   MGH via courier for opinion DDK jam  CO   617  REMOVED_DATE Edited by  REMOVED_DATE    REMOVED_CASE_ID  PAT MURJ REMOVED_DATE    REMOVED_CASE_ID  PAT DORD REMOVED_DATE    REMOVED_CASE_ID  PAT INDMCODES   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    Left breast  NO Needle  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    Left breast  NO Atypia suspicio  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    Left breast  NO For BREANBX  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID     BREANBX Microinvasive t BREANBX  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID     BREANBX Atypical hyperp BREANBX  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID     BREANBX Fibrocystic dis BREANBX  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID     BREANBX Carcinoma  NOS BREANBX  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID     BREANBX Atypical lobula Edited by  REMOVED_DATE    REMOVED_CASE_ID  PAT DORDCOPIES TO  MELNICK AVROHM N  M D   REMOVED_CASE_ID  WASHINGTON ST  STE 570 NEWTON  MA  REMOVED_CASE_ID   617 964  REMOVED_CASE_ID  SELLAND DONNA LEE M D  NEWTON WELLESLEY RADIOLOGY NEWTON  MA  REMOVED_CASE_ID   617 243  REMOVED_CASE_ID ICD CODES  REMOVED_DATE   Edited by  REMOVED_DATE    REMOVED_CASE_ID  PAT DORDMARKERS  Breast Disease Related  Presented at Breast CA Conf   Consult Case Out  Consult Case Return  Kodachromes in file  Tumor case Edited by  REMOVED_DATE    REMOVED_CASE_ID  PAT MURJ REMOVED_DATE    REMOVED_CASE_ID  PAT DORD REMOVED_DATE    REMOVED_CASE_ID  PAT MURJ REMOVED_DATE    REMOVED_CASE_ID  PAT INDM REMOVED_DATE    REMOVED_CASE_ID  PAT MURJPROCEDURES  IMMUNOCYTO  04 28 05  REMOVED_CASE_ID  PAT DORD   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   04 28 05  REMOVED_CASE_ID  PAT DORD  Edited by  REMOVED_DATE    REMOVED_CASE_ID  PAT MURJ REMOVED_DATE    REMOVED_CASE_ID  PAT DORD REMOVED_DATE    REMOVED_CASE_ID  PAT DORDTISSUES  Breast  needle biopsy   left Edited by  REMOVED_DATE    REMOVED_CASE_ID  PAT MURJFinal  SAMPSO