aining of 100 routinely processed breast carcinomas with determinations of progesterone receptors by a hormone binding assay  Sixty five percent of tumors that stained with this antibody contained progesterone receptors by the biochemical assay  and 98  of tumors that failed to stain lacked the receptors  THE RESULTS OF IMMUNOHISTOCHEMICAL STAINING FOR PROGESTERONE RECEPTOR PROTEIN ARE FOR INVESTIGATIONAL USE ONLY  THEY MUST NOT BE USED FOR CLINICAL PURPOSES  Added by  Frederick Carl Koerner  M D  Signed On  24 REMOVED_MONTH 93 at 13 51 GROSS DESCRIPTION  Received fresh in the Frozen Section Lab   labeled REMOVE_PATIENT_NAME  patient s name  unit number  and  Left breast  1  upper outer quadrant lesion  is a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 1 4cm piece of breast tissue with localizing wire in place  The mammogram accompanies the specimen  The specimen is inked and sectioned to reveal two firm foci each less than 1cm in size  One focus is submitted in A and B  the other in C through E  and the remainder of this specimen in F through J  No frozen section performed  Received fresh in the Frozen Section Lab   labeled REMOVE_PATIENT_NAME  patient s name  unit number  and  Left breast  2  are a 4 x REMOVED_DATE x 1 2cm piece of breast with localizing wire and a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 0 6cm piece of breast  The larger specimen is inked and sectioned to reveal several irregular firm foci each less than 1cm in diameter  submitted in K through L  and the remainder submitted in M through S  The smaller fragment is inked and sectioned to reveal unremarkable breast tissue  all submitted in T through U  No frozen section done  Resident  Douglas B  Flieder  MD     INTRA OPERATIVE DIAGNOSIS     GROSS DIAGNOSIS   1 LEFT BREAST   NO LESION LARGE ENOUGH TO FREEZE  GROSS DIAGNOSIS   2 LEFT BREAST   NO LESION LARGE ENOUGH TO FREEZE  AWAIT PERMANENTS  O R  Resident  Lisa H  Lerner  MD O R  Diagnosis by  Judith Ann Ferry  M D                                                            