Accession Number   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status  Final  Type  Surgical Pathology  Pathology Report   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   Massachusetts General Hospital  Boston  MA  REMOVED_CASE_ID  Tel  617  REMOVED_DATE    Molecular Pathology Report   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  FISH Analysis      Accession     REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME  H   DOB  REMOVED_DATE  Age  47   Sex  F  MRN   REMOVED_CASE_ID   Institution  MGH  Location  YAW 9  BREAST ONC  Date of Collection  REMOVED_DATE  Date of Accession  REMOVED_DATE  Reported  REMOVED_DATE 21 56  Results To   Steven Jay Isakoff MD  PHD  Mari Mino Kenudson MD    CLINICAL HISTORY   Breast Adenocarcinoma Metastatic to Hematopoietic and Lymphoid Tissue  Lymph  Node     SPECIMENS RECEIVED   A  Molecular test for  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID     GROSS DESCRIPTION   1 block from Massachusetts General Hospital  the archived specimen was retrieved  and reviewed for adequacy    SLIDE BLOCK DESCRIPTION    REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID             TEST    REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  FISH ANALYSIS    INDICATION FOR TEST  Breast Adenocarcinoma Metastatic to Hematopoietic and  Lymphoid Tissue  Lymph Node     SPECIMEN S  TESTED   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   Massachusetts General Hospital  Boston  MA   United States     RESULTS     Fibroblast Growth Factor Receptor   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   copy number analysis      REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   Reference Probe Copy Number Ratio  REMOVED_DATE    INTERPRETATION     The FISH data are consistent with NO amplification of the  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  gene     TEST INFORMATION   Aberrations involving the fibroblast growth factor receptor 1   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   gene have  been noted in tumors of multiple types  including certain lung  breast  ovarian   skeletal muscle  melanocytic and hematopoietic neoplasms   1  Amplification of  the  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  gene was recently identified as a significant and common finding  22   by FISH  in a cohort of squamous cell carcinomas of the lung   2     The assay performed on this patient uses fluorescence in situ hybridization   FISH  on formalin fixed paraffin embedded tumor specimens to analyze  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  gene  amplification status  Briefly  5 micron sections of formalin fixed  paraffin embedded tumor material were prepared and an H E section reviewed to  select regions for hybridization that contain a majority of tumor cells  A  dual color FISH assay was performed using the 08 REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  locus probe   chromosome 8p11  Leica Biosystems  and a copy number probe  centromere 8   Abbott Vysis 06 REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  or 06 REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID       Signal quantitation was used to generate a  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  centromere 8 ratio   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  to  Centromere 8 ratios are interpreted as follows  REMOVED_DATE to REMOVED_DATE is considered  borderline for amplification  REMOVED_DATE to REMOVED_DATE is considered low level amplification   and greater than REMOVED_DATE or clustered  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  signals that are too numerous to count  would be considered highly amplified  CPT codes for this assay are  REMOVED_CASE_ID    morphometric analysis  in situ hybridization  each probe  manual  x 1     REFERENCES   1  Turner N and Grose R  Fibroblast growth factor signaling  from  development to cancer  Nature Reviews Cancer 10 116   REMOVED_CASE_ID     2  Weiss J  Sos  M  et al  Frequent and focal  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  amplification  associates with therapeutically tractable  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  dependency in squamous cell lung  cancer  Sci Transl Med 2 62  62ra93   REMOVED_CASE_ID       This test was developed  and its performance characteristics were determined by  the MGH Center for Integrated Diagnostics  It has not been cleared or approved  by the U S  Food and Drug Administration  The FDA has determined that such  clearance or approval is not necessary  This test is used for clinical purposes   Pursuant to the requirements of CLIA 